> I am writing up a methods appendix for a report. The first part of this section discusses risk modeling:
> ```
> \subsection{Risk modeling}
> 
> We represent “missing risks” as all climate damages not explicitly captured in our sectoral channels. They are approximated using two complementary methods: one derived from the UK Climate Change Risk Assessment 3 (CCRA3) monetary valuations \citep{watkiss2021monetary}, and one based on a simple fraction of known risks following \citet{nordhaus2013climate}. These are treated as alternative models, and we sample from both in the overall Monte Carlo analysis.
> 
> In the CCRA3-based method, we start from the ranges reported for each risk area in \citetalias{watkiss2021monetary}, infer indicative means and standard errors, and fit simple quadratic damage functions (anchored at zero damages at $0\,^\circ$C warming) across the reported time slices, all in a common price and temperature baseline. Sectors that overlap with our explicit channels are used only for comparison; the remaining CCRA3 sectors are treated as candidates for missing risks. For partly documented or undocumented sectors, we impute missing time slices or whole profiles by scaling or copying from better-documented CCRA3 sectors within a Monte Carlo framework. We then account for dependence and double-counting using the copula-based “rapid assessment” method of \citet{rising2022missing}, with correlation and overlap matrices derived from shared hazards and CCRA3 documentation. This yields a simulated distribution of aggregate missing-risk damages, which we summarise as a quadratic damage function in global mean temperature.
> 
> In the second method, we estimate missing risks simply as a fixed fraction of known risks. For each Monte Carlo iteration, we randomly select one available model per sector, sum these to obtain total “known” damages (with adjustments where there is known double-counting, e.g.\ drought), and set missing risks equal to 25\% of that total, following \citet{nordhaus2013climate}. This produces a second distribution of missing-risk damages that scales with the size of known impacts.
> 
> In the final aggregation, missing risks are sampled by drawing from the Monte Carlo pools generated by both methods, so that headline results reflect both a CCRA3-based extrapolation and a proportional-to-known-risks view of unmodelled damages. Further details on the copula construction, imputation rules, and integration with sectoral damages are provided in \citet{rising2026comprehensive}.
