> \subsection{Stochastic annual variability and extreme events}
> 
> To reflect uncertainty from year-to-year climate fluctuations and the possibility of rare, high-impact events, we extend the reduced-form missing-risk damage functions to a stochastic annual framework.
> 
> We begin with the pooled missing-risk damage functions described above, expressed as percentage losses in global GDP at discrete temperature levels $T \in \{1, 1.5, 2, 4\}\,^\circ$C for each Monte Carlo draw $m$. These functions are defined for each global mean temperature level and Monte Carlo draw, and are treated as describing the expected fraction of global GDP lost in a representative year at that temperature.
> 
> We then combine these functions with annual projections of global-mean surface air temperature under two representative climate futures. For each year $t$ from 2025 to 2075, global temperatures are taken from an ensemble of physically based climate projections, distinguishing a “Most Likely” emissions–climate trajectory and a “High Risk” pathway with higher warming (centred on a medium–high emissions scenario). Each climate model realisation is treated as one Monte Carlo draw of the annual temperature path. For each year and realisation, we map the continuous projected temperature to the closest of the four evaluated temperature levels, and evaluate the corresponding missing-risk damage draw. Aggregating across all realisations and Monte Carlo draws yields an annual distribution of missing-risk damages for each year and scenario, which we summarise by the mean and 5–95\% quantiles. We interpret this as an estimate of expected annual damages (EAD) from missing risks, conditional on the evolving climate state and the underlying uncertainty in the reduced-form damage functions.
> 
> To represent low-frequency, high-impact extremes, we superimpose an additional stochastic component on these annual damages under the High Risk pathway. For three future evaluation dates $t \in \{2030, 2040, 2050\}$, we consider a decadal window $[t, t+9]$ of climate outcomes under the same ensemble of projections. Within each decadal window and for each ensemble member, we construct an “extreme-year” outcome by summing two independent realisations of missing-risk damages: one based on the baseline decadal temperature for that year, and a second drawn from a temperature-dependent distribution that favours outcomes closer to the realised temperature but allows higher or lower temperature levels with exponentially decaying probability. This second term is intended to emulate the additional impact of a rare, extreme manifestation of the same underlying hazards (e.g.\ coincident shocks, compound events, or tail realisations of impact processes) rather than a distinct hazard category.
> 
> For each ensemble member, we then identify the year within the decadal window with the largest combined (baseline plus extreme) missing-risk damage, and take that “worst year” impact as one realisation of a decadal extreme. Repeating this across all ensemble members yields a Monte Carlo distribution of 1-in-10-year worst outcomes, which we treat as a stylised approximation to a low-probability, high-impact tail event relevant for decision-making. We summarise this distribution for each evaluation decade by its mean and 5–95\% quantiles.
> 
> Figure~\ref{fig:missriks-dataset} shows the resulting time series of expected annual missing-risk damages under the two climate futures, together with the decadal “extreme-year” outcomes for the High Risk pathway. The expected annual damages increase over time with global temperature, and the extreme-year estimates trace out the upper tail of possible losses when compounding shocks are allowed for within each decade.
> 
> \begin{figure}[t]
>   \centering
>   \includegraphics[width=0.8\textwidth]{figures/adapt/missriks/dataset.pdf}
>   \caption{Expected annual damages from missing risks (solid lines with shaded 5–95\% intervals) under “Most Likely” and “High Risk” climate futures, and illustrative decadal extreme-year outcomes (error bars) under the High Risk pathway. Damages are expressed as a percentage of global GDP.}
>   \label{fig:missriks-dataset}
> \end{figure}
