> \subsection{Stochastic annual variability and extreme events}
> 
> We extend the missing-risks damage function to reflect interannual variability and a stylised representation of rare, high-impact years, using the same climate scenarios and principles described elsewhere in this report.
> 
> For each year $t$ from 2025 to 2075 and each climate model realisation (treated as one Monte Carlo draw), we take the projected global mean temperature and map it to the nearest evaluated level $T \in \{1, 1.5, 2, 4\}\,^\circ\text{C}$. We then sample one Monte Carlo draw of missing-risk damages at that temperature and summarise, across all realisations, the mean and 5–95\% interval. This yields a time series of expected annual damages (EAD) from missing risks under the Most Likely and High Risk scenarios, consistent with the temperature evolution in each case.
> 
> To illustrate low-probability extremes under the High Risk pathway, we construct “1-in-200” outcomes for three reference years $t \in \{2030, 2040, 2050\}$. For each reference year, we consider a decade of projected temperatures and, for each climate realisation, form an extreme-year loss as the sum of (i) the baseline missing-risk damage associated with that year’s temperature and (ii) an additional independent draw from the missing-risks damage function, where the temperature level is sampled from an exponential-like weighting around the realised temperature. We select, within each decade and realisation, the year with the largest combined loss and summarise the resulting Monte Carlo distribution by its mean and 5–95\% interval. These extremes are reported as indicative 1-in-200 outcomes.
> 
> Figure~\ref{fig:missriks-dataset} presents the resulting EAD time series and associated extreme-year estimates for missing risks.
> 
> \begin{figure}[t]
>   \centering
>   \includegraphics[width=0.8\textwidth]{figures/adapt/missriks/dataset.pdf}
>   \caption{Missing-risk damages as a share of global GDP: expected annual damages (EAD) under the Most Likely and High Risk scenarios, and illustrative 1-in-200 outcomes under the High Risk scenario.}
>   \label{fig:missriks-dataset}
> \end{figure}
